1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,635
This is PC Guardian AI,

2
00:00:02,635 --> 00:00:06,835
a portable Windows diagnosis and safe repair prototype.

3
00:00:06,835 --> 00:00:13,090
Today's demonstration uses test fixtures, CPU rules, and a mock backend.

4
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:15,175
The app runs no trained model,

5
00:00:15,175 --> 00:00:18,235
and every repair is simulated without changing this computer.

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,305
The scan displays three issues: a stopped Print Spooler,

7
00:00:24,305 --> 00:00:28,015
low disk space, and a DNS problem.

8
00:00:28,015 --> 00:00:32,630
The evidence and suggested next steps appear alongside the findings.

9
00:00:32,630 --> 00:00:35,435
Missing information stays unknown.

10
00:00:35,435 --> 00:00:38,575
A failed network check is evidence about that test,

11
00:00:38,575 --> 00:00:42,414
rather than proof that the computer has completely lost Internet access.

12
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:50,815
The only permitted repair in this prototype

13
00:00:50,815 --> 00:00:53,065
is starting the Print Spooler.

14
00:00:53,065 --> 00:00:57,005
Disk cleanup and network configuration remain manual.

15
00:00:57,005 --> 00:00:59,460
I begin with dry-run enabled.

16
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:03,865
This previews the allowlisted action without changing service state.

17
00:01:03,865 --> 00:01:07,980
Future model output must never become a command for Windows to execute.

18
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:16,685
Now I turn off dry-run and request the simulated repair.

19
00:01:16,685 --> 00:01:19,445
The confirmation explains the action and reminds us

20
00:01:19,445 --> 00:01:23,075
that starting the service could resume queued print jobs.

21
00:01:23,075 --> 00:01:27,280
First, I choose No. Nothing runs.

22
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:30,025
I request it again and choose Yes.

23
00:01:30,025 --> 00:01:33,975
This approval allows the demo to update its simulated service state,

24
00:01:33,975 --> 00:01:35,672
while leaving the host untouched.

25
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,550
Approval is followed by verification.

26
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:48,700
The app checks the service again and reports

27
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:51,410
that the simulated Spooler is running.

28
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:55,750
A request to repair something is not itself evidence of recovery.

29
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:58,360
The result must reflect the follow-up check,

30
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:02,387
including any permission failure, execution error, or uncertainty.

31
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:07,810
The readable report brings the findings,

32
00:02:07,810 --> 00:02:11,005
repair result, and verification together.

33
00:02:11,005 --> 00:02:13,095
It gives the user or a support technician

34
00:02:13,095 --> 00:02:15,130
a record of what happened.

35
00:02:15,130 --> 00:02:19,845
The application also writes structured JSON and a local audit log.

36
00:02:19,845 --> 00:02:22,135
Those files support later review,

37
00:02:22,135 --> 00:02:24,658
but we do not claim that the log is tamper-proof.

38
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:33,100
A new scan shows two remaining issues:

39
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:36,240
low disk space and the DNS problem.

40
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:38,630
The Spooler finding is gone.

41
00:02:38,630 --> 00:02:42,075
Resolving one issue does not mark every other finding as healthy.

42
00:02:46,585 --> 00:02:49,588
Next, USB storage will carry the software,

43
00:02:49,589 --> 00:02:53,083
with UGen300 planned for supported model inference.

44
00:02:53,084 --> 00:02:56,205
Windows retains control of permissions and repairs.

45
00:02:56,206 --> 00:02:58,849
Hardware integration and performance remain unvalidated.
